RELEVANCE RULES (they apply to the relevance tier everywhere):
- Find the profile interest that best matches what the paper is mainly about and note which section lists it (core / strong / methodological angles / excluded).
- Every item listed under core counts as core. Do not rank some core items above others: if the profile lists segmentation, tokenizers, post-training and agents as core, a promptable-segmentation paper or a coding-agent paper is as core as a VLM-reasoning paper.
- Methods, analyses, diagnostic studies, benchmarks, datasets and data pipelines count equally: a paper that benchmarks or analyzes a core topic is as core as one that proposes a method for it. An efficiency technique applied to a core topic still counts for that topic.
- Agents on any platform (coding, web, GUI, mobile, embodied) are agents, not "application domains".
- The application-domain limit applies only to domains the profile does not list or explicitly excludes (e.g. medical imaging, remote sensing, one specific human language).
- Relevance says how close the topic is; whether the work is good belongs to novelty, evidence and reusability, not to relevance.
